 put some olive oil into  it and toss your shrimp heads into there  you're going to sauté the shrimp heads  for about a minute or two just until the  shrimp heads get pink and you're going  to instantly smell the flavors of the  shrimp now with shrimp and seafood in  general the majority of the fat aka the  flavor is going to come from the actual  the head of the of the shrimp that's  where like all this stuff is that now I  know what you're thinking it might be  kind of gross but this is how it's done  so you're going to sauté the shrimp it's  gonna bring out the flavors introduce  the crab to it now for you guys that  don't want to deal with making your own  seafood stock that's fine  stick with the chicken stock or a beef  stock it all works out you can use water  if you want but I don't I don't  recommend that once you have two shrimp  in the crab cooking for about two  minutes you want to introduce your  chicken broth to the concoction-- place  it on high bring it up to a boil I want  to make this stock a little sweet in  terms of the aroma in order to do that  I'm gonna add some of those carrots that  we have just coarsely chopped there  we're not eating the carrot so it's just  for flavoring chop it up  we're in toss it into the stock once  it's boiling you want to reduce the heat  bring it back down to the simmer and let  that sit for the next two hours while  you prep your jambalaya we got to  introduce a little bit of fat like I  said that is flavor  so let's start off with the bacon three  strips of bacon you can actually use six  strips of bacon if you want I personally  would use six strips but I'm trying to  I'm trying to stay lean so I'm not gonna  go too crazy with the bacon you're gonna  want to cut the bacon into like one-inch  little squares I guess toss into that  new pot and let that sizzle and let that  cook and let that Brown you want to  start your jambalaya with some nice  bacon fat some nice bacon flavor now  while that's browning and we're sweating  the crap out of the bacon start off with  the onion and let's chop that up now I  got to introduce you to the Holy Trinity  now the Holy Trinity is the base for a  lot of regional  is from Louisiana your Holy Trinity is  going to consist onion bell peppers and  celery that's exactly what we're going  to add next dice it up nice even pieces  you don't want certain pieces larger  than others because you want to make  every bite of jambalaya uniform in your  mouth it's all about proportion and  flavors with this dish and that makes a  perfect bite I'm using about double the  amount of vegetables that you're going  to see in most jambalaya dishes just  because I love vegetables in my opinion  the more the merrier and it's just going  to make your jambalaya that much more of  a slam-dunk of flavors now when it comes  to rice we're gonna need two cups of  rice white rice make sure you wash your  rice we want to get rid of that starchy  how do we layer that's on the outside of  the grain or else your your rice is  going to start clumping together and get  too starchy once you're done with that  toss that into the pot and you want to  toast the rice from that point on and  start introducing those flavors coming  from the Holy Trinity and from the bacon  and start infusing those flavors into  the grains of rice you want to make sure  that you keep stirring this the rice is  going to start sticking to the bottom if  it sticks to the bottom you're gonna  have really burnt up rice at the bottom  of your pot and it's gonna be hard to  wash now while the rice is toasting  we're gonna introduce the rest of the  vegetables so we're gonna take the four  to six garlic cloves that we have you're  going to dice that up as well toss that  in take some parsley just a nice handful  of parsley and dice that up into nice  little shreds and sprinkle that in last  but not least the green onions now once  you have the green onions chopped up you  want to use about 3/4 of that 75% of the  green onions goes into the pot and at  this point you're going to start  smelling all those aromatics all those  vegetables kind of just fusing together  and creating this beautiful flavor this  beautiful aroma from here we're gonna  start introducing our proteins so we're  gonna take this for andouille sausages  once again we're going to just cut them  up into nice little coins about a  quarter of an inch thick and dice those  up and you know toss them and just like  everything else  now these end dewey sausages they're of  french origin it is a smoked pork  sausage so once you have the andouille  sausages give it a 1 nice stir really  dig into the bottom and really get all  the bottom stuff up  you don't want it to burn smoke flavor  for then do we sausages is one thing  burnt flavor from the rice getting stuck  at the bottom that's a whole nother  thing we do not want that our jumbo is  looking really really really green which  is great but now we're gonna add the  color of passion to it some Reds so  we're going to start introducing our  tomatoes let's go ahead and add our 1  can of tomatoes and 2 tablespoons of  tomato paste once we have the tomatoes  add it in our seafood stock should be  done or right about now should be about  one or two hours so we're gonna go ahead  and extract 2 cups a seafood stock now  this can't be kind of tricky jambalaya  is one of those dishes you can easily  add too much liquids to and it's going  to turn into this mushy gushy kind of  texture where the rice grains are  completely gone we don't want that since  we added so many vegetables there's  actually quite a bit of liquid and  moisture coming from the vegetables  still and along with those tomatoes that  we just added there's plenty of liquid  to go around so we don't want to drown  our rice with too much of this stock  that we made a little bit goes a long  way so we're going to only use 2 cups of  the seafood stock and once you've added  your liquids the next thing you want to  do is add some spices and some salt and  pepper to taste now I usually like to  throw in some paprika for some color and  then I usually like to also add some  oregano flakes as well just to kind of  give it that extra complexity of flavors  and we're gonna bring that up to a boil  and once at a boil I usually like to  give it one last stir just so that you  pick up all the bottom stuff up just to  keep it moving and never let it settle  for too long or else it's gonna burn now  once you give it a nice stir we're gonna  finally place the lid on top up into  this point we haven't touched the lid  it's been open this is the time when we  put the lid on we're gonna trap all that  heat and moisture in there and really  let those flavors now to really really  let it cook into the rice and let the  rice start to soak in all the flavors  now to its very very core now you're  gonna want to cook your rest for about  30 minutes you used to remember the  shrimp that we had from the beginning of  the cook let's get those out again now  while the rice is cooking I usually like  to take this time to cut the shrimp's in  half we're into thirds depending on the  size of the shrimp and since we're  waiting for 30 minutes I'm  take this time to also devein the shrimp  so now take the lid off you're going to  see this amazing looking jambalaya it's  going to start taking shape now take the  shrimp we're going to dump the shrimp in  as soon as the shrimp goes in we take  off the heat turn off your stove we're  going to be using the residual heat in  the pot to finish up our jambalaya the  easiest mistake to make at this point is  to stir the shrimp in you don't want to  stir the rice at this point is going to  be very very soft and tender and the  more you stir the more of the rice grain  itself is going to start breaking and  once it starts breaking with all that  moisture and liquid in there it's going  to start turning into a mush and you  definitely do not want that so when you  add your shrimp in what you want to do  is take a ladle and actually dig deep to  the bottom and bring it back up dig to  the bottom bring it back up  he's very gentle with the rice and kind  of let the rice smother the shrimp and  let that residual heat cook the shrimp  remember those remaining green onions  that we chopped up and spring those back  out and we're going to just kind of  sprinkle them into the pot and let those  green onions kind of state you don't  want to cook those green onions too much  so once again we're also using the  residual heat from the pot to get those  green onions worked into the rest of the  jambalaya and then once you have that  done there's only one thing left to do  the most beautiful ingredient that we're  going to introduce the pot is butter  we're going to use about two tablespoons  of butter I know I said I'm on a diet I  didn't want to use too much bacon but at  the end of the day that butter just  really kind of just brings everything  together it just makes it very cohesive  it's just a nice little coating of  butter that creaminess gently work that  into the rest of the jambalaya and make  sure it coats as many grains of rice as  possible and just kind of keep it moving  for a little bit for you very very  gentle voila we have jambalaya  the best jambalaya you're ever gonna  taste good mmm is good  I surprised myself they're eating my  food it's yummy mm-hmm I like this lazy  seasoning don't start with me and  they're having a very good meal beat it  No

